00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.500
Together, these fully
managed and flexible products

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.420
help developers connect
the cloud with less code.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.370
In our demo today,
you will see many

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:34.750
of these products in action--

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.870
specifically, Eventarc,
Workflows, Cloud Run,

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:41.190
and Cloud Functions.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:44.070
So what do we mean by
event-driven architecture?

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.830
First, let's talk about
the modern application.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.470
Here's an example of
a simple application

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.900
with a complex web of service
teams working together

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.520
towards a common goal.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.970
This application is
somewhat complicated

00:01:57.970 --> 00:01:59.920
with interactions
and dependencies

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.650
that can become a
drag on velocity.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.680
But in reality, today
your application

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.699
is more likely to look like
this, an extremely complex web

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:11.410
of point-to-point
interactions creating

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.660
a significant number
of dependencies

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.230
and points of failure.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.360
As applications
expand horizontally,

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.310
connections become
even more complex

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.040
and difficult to understand.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.570
While microservice
patterns provide

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.420
a great deal of
advantages, they also

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.890
come with a great
deal of complexity.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.500
By contrast,
event-driven architecture

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.350
produces a solution
for this problem.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.630
It provides the ability
to reduce dependencies

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.190
and complexity in
your application

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.800
while still allowing
the creation of more

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.510
and more microservices.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.970
Event-driven architecture allows
true separation of concern.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.980
A producer need not
know how an event will

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.440
be consumed or take
on any dependency

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.840
from a downstream service.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:58.730
Similarly, the
consumer only needs

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.280
to know that the event will
be raised and understand

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.380
how to utilize it,
none of the details

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.890
of the upstream service.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.750
With this loosely
coupled approach,

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.870
we eliminate hard
dependencies between services

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:14.480
and eliminate that
drag on velocity.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.000
In a traditional
application, you can only

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.850
scale one way, vertically.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.740
With event-driven
architecture, a developer

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.690
is easily able to
scale an application

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.440
as performance needs increase.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.960
Furthermore, imagine
a scenario where

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.270
you're adding a new service
to an existing application.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.950
In a point-to-point
signaling model,

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.690
upstream services
must be updated

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.870
in order to signal the
downstream services.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.120
However, in an
event-driven pattern,

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.670
users are easily able
to add a new service

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.280
from existing events
without updating

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.440
the upstream application at all.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.060
This helps to
eliminate that spider

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.310
web of concerns and
point-to-point interactions

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:54.210
substantially.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.170
Eventarc is Google's
solution to this problem.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.940
It is a managed
eventing system that

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.810
makes building event-driven
serverless applications easy.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:08.110
Simply select the Google
Cloud service or Pub/Sub

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:11.000
topic you're interested
in as a source,

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.360
define the filter
parameters, and choose

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.830
the target you wish to invoke.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:18.620
We do use Pub/Sub in the
background as a transport layer

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:22.440
to benefit from its superior
reliability and observability,

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.590
but we take care of all
the details of setup.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.360
Simply create that
trigger, and we'll

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.060
take care of the event
ingress, the filtering,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.570
and the delivery of events
to your desired target.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:38.160
Lastly, we use the CNCF
Standard cloud events format.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.890
And because of this, it's
easy to understand and marshal

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.230
the details of your
events in order

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.020
to more intelligently
drive application behavior.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.350
Now I'd like to share
an important customer

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:55.170
story from one of our
customers, Vivint.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:58.150
Vivint is an
exciting IoT company.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.040
They currently power
the smart home for

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.900
over 1.8 million customers.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.560
Their portfolio of devices is
impressive, offering smart home

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.540
bundles that cover
everything from cameras

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:12.890
to locks to important sensors
such as flood sensors.

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:15.200
As these devices are
important for ensuring

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.290
your home is running
smoothly and safely,

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.930
latency and reliability
of the backend system

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.660
is extremely important for
creating the right customer

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.070
experience.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.760
Let's talk a bit about the
challenge they were facing.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:32.090
In 2020, Vivint was presented
with an exciting business

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:33.230
opportunity.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:35.630
They intended to offer a
standalone doorbell camera

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:38.170
outside of their
smart home bundle.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.390
This was an interesting
technical challenge.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:41.980
Their core system,
you see, was built

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:45.010
around the concept of bundles,
so this standalone offering

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:48.080
was a significant
departure from that model.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.190
Lastly, time to market
really mattered.

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:53.740
There was a limited
window they had in

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.210
which to make this
happen in order

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:59.280
to address this
fast-moving market segment.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.800
Here's what they
built. By leveraging

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.670
events and serverless
technology,

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.670
they were able to separate
the components needed

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:08.070
to run the camera application
from the core platform

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.320
without having to
rewrite everything,

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.830
and without adding any risk
or instability to the user

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.840
experience of either
the new application

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.660
or their core offering.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.570
Let's examine in detail the
architecture used for their use

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:25.690
case, or one of their
use cases specifically,

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:29.770
an image processing pipeline
for their camera application.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:33.190
Images were loaded from
the camera to a GCS bucket.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:35.140
This invoked a Cloud
Function or Cloud

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:38.440
Run for Anthos Service, which
analyzed the images using

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.920
the Computer Vision API in
order to categorize and annotate

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.590
appropriately.

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.570
The results were
updated in a BigQuery

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:48.870
data set for use later.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.780
And the frontend platform was
signaled via an integration

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.220
with Pub/Sub.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.100
Firebase FCM was used in
order to send notifications

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.470
to the mobile app, and
their core platform

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:00.390
received the event
for historical reasons

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:03.040
and tracking.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.730
The result, Eventarc and
GCP surveillance technology

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.540
allowed them to accelerate time
to value and time to market.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:12.880
Not only did these products
help solve their problem

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:15.340
but did so in a way
that would scale,

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:17.800
and by achieving
separation of concern

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:22.950
without risking their
core technology.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:24.770
To dig into some of
the secondary benefits,

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:26.520
this provided a number
of things that were

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.640
really interesting to them.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:31.790
First, it decreased the time
to market, as I mentioned.

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:34.190
The backend system was
developed and ready to go

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.760
earlier than expected.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.520
They were also able to
implement continuous deployment.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:42.340
They deployed new code
after every two-week sprint.

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:46.590
This is an improvement over the
previous quarterly OTA updates.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:49.470
By using event processing
in the same location,

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.170
they were also able to--
sorry, in the same location

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:53.820
as the notification
service, they

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:58.710
were also able to
decrease latency by 78%.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.430
And finally, built entirely
on managed serverless

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.350
and orchestration
products, the new solution

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:07.830
was scalable even in the
most successful scenarios.

00:08:10.668 --> 00:08:12.710
Vivint didn't have to
worry about scalability out

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:14.690
of the gate, as they
were able to rest

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:17.450
assured in the confidence
of Google Cloud's ability

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:19.990
to scale.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:22.300
Hopefully, Vivint's
story helps exemplify

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:25.180
how Eventarc and Google Cloud
can help accelerate time

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:28.510
to value for your organization.

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:31.750
To help understand how this can
work for you in the real world,

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:34.539
Mete has created a demo of
a similar image processing

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:36.510
pipeline.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:39.830
So with this said, I will
hand things over to Mete.

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:41.150
METE ATAMEL: Thanks, Bryan.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:44.750
I'll show you a simplified
image processing pipeline.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.930
In this pipeline, users drop
images in a Cloud Storage

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:49.190
bucket.

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:52.400
These images will be processed
by a chain of Cloud Run

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.470
and Cloud Function services.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:58.370
We use Eventarc to trigger
the pipeline via Cloud Run,

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.830
and Workflows to orchestrate
the services that

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:02.240
process the images.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.280
Let's have a look.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:04.780
OK.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:06.490
Before running the
demo, let's look

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:08.440
at the architecture diagram.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:13.570
First, we have end users saving
some images to an input bucket.

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:16.660
Once the image is saved,
this generates an event,

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:20.140
and this event goes to a
Filter Cloud Run service.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:24.370
Now, this event is routed
using Eventarc's Cloud Storage

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:25.670
trigger.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.420
Now the filter service
will receive the image,

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.860
and it will use Vision API to
determine if it's a safe image

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:33.610
or not.

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:36.280
If the image is
safe, then the filter

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:39.310
starts a Workflow execution.

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:42.190
This Workflow execution,
called image processing,

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:45.310
will call a number
of Cloud Functions.

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:48.190
The first Cloud Function
is called Labeler.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:50.680
Labeler will receive
the image and again, it

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.730
will use Vision API to extract
the labels out of the image.

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:58.000
And it will save them
to an output bucket.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.240
Then Workflows will call
a Resizer Cloud Function.

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:05.350
This will take the image, and
resize it into 400 by 400,

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.480
and save it to that
bucket as well.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.110
And then there will
be another function

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:14.110
called Watermarker that
will receive the labels

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:15.340
from the first function.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:18.490
And it will also receive
the resized image.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:23.170
And it will add a
watermark using the labels

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.760
on the resized image.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:29.350
And then it will save
it to the output bucket.

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:32.170
As you can see, these are
three separate functions,

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:36.250
but they're orchestrated
together using Workflows.

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:38.680
So how do you set
something like this?

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:39.710
We'll take a look.

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:44.380
But before I show you the code
and the setup, all of the code

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:47.680
is on GitHub, and all the
instructions are on GitHub.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.030
So feel free to check
it out yourself as well.

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:54.620
Now first, let's take a
look at what we need to do.

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:56.390
So this is the GitHub page.

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:59.740
And before you begin, you
need to set up the project ID.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:03.340
You need to enable the APIs
that we need with Eventarc

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:04.930
and Workflows and others.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:06.550
Then we need to pick a region.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:08.740
And we need to set
our Cloud Run region

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:12.340
and our Eventarc
location to that region.

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:15.010
And then we need to configure
some service accounts,

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:20.863
because we're going to use
them in Eventarc triggers.

00:11:20.863 --> 00:11:22.780
So we want to make sure
that we have a service

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:25.960
account with
eventarc.eventReceiver role.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:28.390
And we also need a
pubsub.publisher role

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:30.490
for the Cloud Storage trigger.

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:33.130
And we also want to make
sure that our Pub/Sub service

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:36.370
account has the
serviceAccountTokenCreator

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:37.330
role.

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:41.090
Now, once we have all of those--
and I already set all of this--

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:43.840
we need to create storage
buckets for our images.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:47.740
So we create one bucket
for the input images

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:50.300
and another bucket
for output images.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.510
So this is where the images
will be saved by the users,

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:56.140
and this is where the
processing will happen

00:11:56.140 --> 00:12:00.230
and the output images
will be saved as well.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:00.730
All right.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:03.820
Then we can start
deploying our services.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:07.780
If you remember, we had
three Cloud Functions,

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:10.210
Watermarker,
Resizer, and Labeler,

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:13.640
that are orchestrated
together in a workflow.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:16.690
So let's look at
Watermarker first.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:22.000
Watermarker is a C# function,
but it can be in any language.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:25.150
And to look at the code
quickly, what it does

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:31.270
is that first, we read the
bucket name as an environment

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:32.060
variable.

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:33.820
This is the output
bucket where we're

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:36.250
going to save the image
once we process it.

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:39.910
Then we receive HTTP
request in this method.

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:43.970
And from the HTTP request, we
extract a couple of things.

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:46.420
First, we extract the bucket
and the file information

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:48.010
so we can download the image.

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.200
And then we also
extract the labels that

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:52.690
will be passed in by Workflows.

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:56.140
And we're going to use these
labels to add a watermark.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:13:00.820
Then we download the
image from the bucket.

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:04.480
Then in this piece of code, we
use an image processing library

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:10.210
to use the labels and add a
watermark on top of the image.

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:13.750
And then finally,
we save the image

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:17.020
with the watermarks
to the output bucket.

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:17.860
All right.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:24.430
Now, to deploy this service
we just use gcloud deploy.

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:26.500
We specify the
runtime as dotnet3.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:32.200
We make sure that this is
an HTTP triggered function.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:34.930
And we also pass in the
environment variable

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:39.340
for the bucket so that the
function knows how to save--

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:41.570
where to save the image.

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:44.500
So once this is deployed,
we can move on to Resizer.

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:48.100
Resizer is very similar to
the previous Watermarker.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:51.280
But instead of adding a
watermark, it resizes.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:54.280
And we deploy it pretty
much the same way.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:55.960
You can check out
the code if you like.

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:59.110
And then Labeler is, again,
very similar function.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:05.000
Instead of resizing the image,
it extracts the labels out

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:05.810
of the image.

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:08.220
And it uses Vision
API to do that.

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:09.950
So if we take a look
at the code quickly,

00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:12.950
you can see that the Labeler
will receive the bucket

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:15.440
and file information,
then it will

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.770
call this ExtractLabelsAsync
method with the storage

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:20.010
URL of the image.

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:22.280
And if you look
at this method, it

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:26.690
basically uses the
Vision API client

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:29.910
and calls the DetectLabelsAsync
to extract the labels.

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:33.440
Then we order the
labels by score.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:38.760
And we return them in
a list that we use.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:41.330
So once we return the
labels, what we do

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.900
is that we first save
these labels in a text

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:46.970
file to the output bucket.

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:50.660
And then we also extract the
top three of these labels

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:53.970
and return them as
HTTP response data.

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:56.760
And we're going to use this
from Workflows as well.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:57.260
OK.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:14:58.670
So that's Labeler.

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:02.390
And now we need to put
this into an orchestration.

00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:05.120
And we do that with Workflows.

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:09.440
And to define the workflow, we
create a workflow.yaml file.

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:12.720
And then we'll define what
happens in that YAML file.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:15.720
So let's take a look
at that quickly here.

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:21.530
So in workflow.yaml file, first
it receives some arguments.

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:24.380
This will be coming from
our Cloud Run service

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:26.210
that I'm going to show you next.

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:28.220
And in these arguments,
we have the information

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:29.720
about the bucket and the file.

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:35.455
And we also pass in the
URLs or our services.

00:15:35.455 --> 00:15:36.830
Because we don't
want to hardcode

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:39.440
these URLs of these
functions, so instead

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.630
we passed them in as arguments.

00:15:41.630 --> 00:15:42.980
Then we have our steps.

00:15:42.980 --> 00:15:47.778
In the first steps, we log our
bucket and file information

00:15:47.778 --> 00:15:49.820
just to make sure that we
are on the right track.

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:52.880
Than in the label
step, we make a call

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:56.780
to the Labeler URL, which is
the URL of the Labeler function.

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:59.070
And we pass in the
bucket and file.

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:02.900
And then this calls
the Labeler function.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:05.000
And then we get the
response from the function

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.760
and save it to
labelResponse variable,

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:10.380
Then we do the same
with resize step.

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:12.380
So in the resize step,
you just call the Resizer

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:15.050
and capture the response
in resizeResponse.

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:19.260
Then in watermark step,
we call the Watermarker,

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:22.760
but we pass in the bucket
and file information

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:24.680
from the resize step.

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:29.300
This is because we want
to add the watermark

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:32.090
for the resized image, right?

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:36.920
So because of that, we pass
in the resized image's bucket

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:38.360
and file information.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:42.563
And we also pass in the labels
from the label response.

00:16:42.563 --> 00:16:44.480
Because these are the
labels that we extracted

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:47.060
from the Labeler function, so
we are passing them in as well.

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:49.340
And the Watermarker
will use those labels

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:50.630
to add the watermark.

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:55.250
And then in the final step,
we return the HTTP status

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:57.440
code from all the
steps, just to make sure

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:01.140
that our workflow
completed successfully.

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:01.640
OK.

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:02.720
So that's workflow.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:06.650
And to deploy our
workflow, all we need to do

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:09.020
is just use gcloud
workflows deploy.

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:13.099
So let's just copy
this, and go back here,

00:17:13.099 --> 00:17:16.800
and deploy our workflow.

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:18.680
So this will look at
our workflow.yaml.

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:21.650
And now it's deploying
our workflow, which

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:23.960
is very quick, as you can see.

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:24.589
Great.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:27.720
Now the next step is
the filter function.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:29.720
So if you go back to our
architecture diagram,

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:32.690
we deployed our functions,
we deployed our workflows.

00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:35.540
Now we need to deploy the
Filter Cloud Run service.

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:37.220
And we need to
make sure that it's

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:39.320
connected to Cloud
Storage events

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:42.260
using a Cloud Storage trigger.

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:45.320
Now, to deploy the filter,
this is a Cloud Run service.

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:49.430
So it's-- we need a Docker file,
and we need to build and push

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:49.945
the image.

00:17:49.945 --> 00:17:51.320
So that's what we
are doing here.

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:53.810
And then we also deploy
the Cloud Run service

00:17:53.810 --> 00:17:57.080
using gcloud run deploy,
and point to our image,

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:59.790
and pass in some
environment variables.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.150
These variables, like the
project ID, region, workflow

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:07.640
name, this is the information
needed for the workflow.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:09.620
But we are also
passing in the URLs

00:18:09.620 --> 00:18:13.730
or our functions, the
Labeler URL, the Resizer URL.

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.670
That way, we don't need to
hardcode them in our workflow.

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:18.980
Now, this, I already did this.

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:23.450
But now we need to connect Cloud
Run service to Cloud Storage

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:24.140
events.

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:26.720
And we do that with
triggers in Eventarc.

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:29.750
So we create a
trigger name, and then

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:32.420
we just create a trigger
with this command.

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:37.370
Here we are saying that this
trigger connects this Cloud Run

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:38.820
service in this region.

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:41.340
And it's filtering these events.

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:45.380
These events, as you can see,
it's the Cloud Storage object

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:46.190
finalize event.

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:48.920
This is the event that gets
triggered when you save a file

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:51.260
and we're also
filtering on the bucket.

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:54.920
So let's copy this as
well, and go back here

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:58.050
and create our trigger.

00:18:58.050 --> 00:19:01.430
So this will create
the Eventarc trigger.

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:04.250
And now everything
pretty much set.

00:19:04.250 --> 00:19:05.390
We have our workflow.

00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:06.950
We have our Cloud Run.

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:08.900
We have our trigger.

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:12.840
Now, to test, we need to drop
an image and see what happens.

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:17.895
So let's make sure
that our trigger is

00:19:17.895 --> 00:19:19.020
created, that it's created.

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:26.330
And if I do gcloud
eventarc triggers list,

00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:28.340
we can see that the
triggers are running.

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:34.350
Now, to test this out, let's
go to our Cloud Storage bucket.

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:38.250
And you can see that I
have an input bucket.

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.590
So let's upload a
couple of pictures here.

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:42.980
So I will upload one
picture of myself.

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:47.360
And then I'll upload another
beach kind of picture.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:48.530
So let's upload these.

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:51.330
And now this starts
up the whole chain.

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:55.130
And if we go to
the output bucket,

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:57.480
we should start
seeing some images.

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:59.270
So now I will wait a little bit.

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:06.380
So you can see that we have some
labels for the first picture.

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:10.750
So if you click on the labels
and look at the actual file,

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:14.470
these are the labels that were
extracted from my picture.

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:18.220
Now the next one we see is
the picture that's resized.

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:23.740
This is the 400 to 400 picture,
so it's resized to 400 to 400.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:28.570
And finally, the third
picture is the resized image

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:29.860
with the watermark.

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:33.550
And the watermark is
just the top three labels

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:34.540
from the image.

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:37.090
And just to show the
other image as well,

00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:39.760
if you look at
the beach, you see

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:44.200
that it has water, cloud,
and sky as the watermark.

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:47.030
So this concludes the demo.

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:48.080
I hope you enjoyed it.

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:50.055
Thanks very much.

